ange of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ is in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874a00aa-7fa0-594c-a983-1d469c66e3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283791} {:answer/id #uuid "61fc9906-f248-54b2-ac04-53f23ba7913d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283792} {:answer/id #uuid "4501a453-79ed-547a-bf76-f82a63525114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283793} {:answer/id #uuid "95d4acfa-189f-570a-89c0-af5e4f8dcd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283794} {:answer/id #uuid "35b41a1d-7c71-547a-8c5a-f0134c903c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fc9906-f248-54b2-ac04-53f23ba7913d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283792} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e969b59-2bad-5cd6-8e50-dd623e3b7254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b17707f8-d0a8-5f70-818e-f01d27903349" :question/math-academy-id "q-117010" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8389ce-ae7d-5fea-abb6-fc9a00b2c4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283798} {:answer/id #uuid "e76dd8f5-9bf3-5c3a-b1bc-caad25f6f5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283799} {:answer/id #uuid "623834c3-b8e6-590e-b111-d763db5bb9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283800} {:answer/id #uuid "ad670d5c-c4d0-5651-b6c2-0e057f4afcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283801} {:answer/id #uuid "3e12364d-146a-5e6c-87c4-7255bb89af44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad670d5c-c4d0-5651-b6c2-0e057f4afcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283801} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48764ef-6fb9-579d-95d9-5fdc4b4c777b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b0aeba7-e625-5567-bece-07afb787092a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117025" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(1,\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ed9026d-641f-5d92-9ac4-39f717d0d77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109800} {:answer/id #uuid "baa4930f-1a05-59b5-8844-96c74ef8a64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109801} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb40bb0-c19f-5088-a310-cdc929b2c098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109802} {:a